IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `hpr-law`

-- Health Practitioner Regulation (Adoption of National Law) Act 2009 (NSW) -- the questions of the registered health
-- practitioner a complaint is made about in New South Wales.
--
-- SEAT: the registered health practitioner who answers the complaint (a doctor, nurse, dentist, pharmacist or other
-- registered practitioner, often also the defendant to a civil claim over the same treatment). Every question is one
-- that person would ask: "Must the Council suspend me now?", "Can I ask it to review that?", "Can I appeal, and by
-- when?", "Does my appeal stop the decision?", "Can I apply for review of the order?", "Can I register again?", "What is
-- the most I can be fined?", "What if I do not attend the examination?", "Can I have a lawyer?". A second seat beside the
-- complainant's (hpr-seat.l4); both draw on hpr-law.l4, so they cannot disagree about the shared rules. Officials get
-- no seat.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `The practitioner's questions`

§§ `The practitioner's nouns`

DECLARE `Urgent action` IS ONE OF
    `the Council must suspend my registration until the Tribunal deals with it, and refer it to the Tribunal as a complaint`
    `the Council must suspend my registration or impose conditions on it`
    `section 150 does not authorise action against me on these facts`

DECLARE `Review by the Council` IS ONE OF
    `the Council may refuse to reconsider: it thinks my application frivolous or vexatious`
    `the Council must reconsider, and may affirm, vary or set aside its decision`
    `the Council must reconsider, but can only affirm it: no change in my circumstances`

DECLARE `Council decision about me` IS ONE OF
    `a reprimand under Division 3`
    `a suspension under Division 3, or a refusal to end one`
    `conditions imposed under s 127AA or Division 3 or 4, or their alteration`
    `a refusal of my s 150I request to alter or remove conditions`
    `a direction or order at an inquiry at a Council meeting (s 148E)`
    `a refusal of my s 152K request about impairment conditions or suspension`
    `a rejection of my request because it was made in a period when requests were not permitted`
    `another decision`

DECLARE `Order against me` IS ONE OF
    `a prohibition order`
    `a suspension of my registration`
    `a cancellation of my registration, or a disqualification from registration`
    `conditions on my registration`
    `an order made on an earlier review`
    `another order`

DECLARE `Direction I did not follow` IS ONE OF
    `a direction to attend counselling (s 145B(1)(g))`
    `a direction to undergo an examination (s 145E)`
    `a notice to undergo skills testing (s 147C)`

DECLARE `Effect of not complying` IS ONE OF
    `it is evidence that I lack sufficient physical or mental capacity to practise`
    `it is evidence that I lack sufficient skill to practise`
    `the Act draws no such inference`

§§ `Urgent action by the Council -- ss 150, 150A` -- arises when the Council moves to protect the public

-- s 150(1): "A Council must, if at any time it is satisfied it is appropriate to do so for the protection of the health
-- or safety of any person or persons ... or if satisfied the action is otherwise in the public interest -- (a) by order
-- suspend [the] registration; or (b) by order impose ... conditions". (2) A suspension lasts until "(a) the complaint
-- ... is disposed of; (b) the suspension is ended by the Council". (3) If satisfied the practitioner "has contravened a
-- critical compliance order or condition, the Council must (a) suspend the ... registration until a complaint ... is
-- dealt with by the Tribunal; and (b) refer the matter to the Tribunal as a complaint." (4) With or without a complaint.
@export Must the NSW Council suspend my registration or put conditions on it now, to protect the public (s 150)?
GIVEN `the Council is satisfied it is needed for protection or the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Council is satisfied that suspending you or imposing conditions is appropriate to protect anyone's health or safety, or is otherwise in the public interest
      `the Council is satisfied I breached a critical compliance order or condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Council is satisfied you breached an order or condition made "critical compliance" (one whose breach means cancellation)
GIVETH AN `Urgent action`
`Must the Council act against me now` MEANS
    IF `the Council is satisfied I breached a critical compliance order or condition`
    THEN `the Council must suspend my registration until the Tribunal deals with it, and refer it to the Tribunal as a complaint`
    ELSE IF `the Council is satisfied it is needed for protection or the public interest`
         THEN `the Council must suspend my registration or impose conditions on it`
         ELSE `section 150 does not authorise action against me on these facts`

-- s 150A(1): a practitioner "may apply to a Council for the review of a decision of the Council under section 150";
-- (2) the Council "(a) may refuse to reconsider its decision if, in the Council's opinion, the application is frivolous
-- or vexatious; or (b) must otherwise reconsider its decision"; (3) it may "(a) affirm or vary the decision; or (b) set
-- it aside"; (4) it "may vary or set aside a decision only if the Council is satisfied there has been a change in the
-- [practitioner's] circumstances that justifies [it]".
@export I have asked the Council to review its urgent suspension or conditions: what must it do (s 150A)?
GIVEN `the Council thinks my application frivolous or vexatious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Council considers your review application frivolous or vexatious
      `the Council is satisfied my circumstances have changed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Council is satisfied there has been a change in your circumstances that justifies varying or setting aside its decision
GIVETH A `Review by the Council`
`What must the Council do on my review application` MEANS
    IF `the Council thinks my application frivolous or vexatious`
    THEN `the Council may refuse to reconsider: it thinks my application frivolous or vexatious`
    ELSE IF `the Council is satisfied my circumstances have changed`
         THEN `the Council must reconsider, and may affirm, vary or set aside its decision`
         ELSE `the Council must reconsider, but can only affirm it: no change in my circumstances`

§§ `Appeals -- ss 158, 158A, 159, 159B, 161, 161B` -- arises once a Council or a Committee has decided

-- s 159(1): "A person may appeal to the Tribunal against" a Council's reprimand (a1), suspension or refusal to end one
-- (a), conditions under s 127AA or Division 3 or 4 or their alteration (b), a refusal on a s 150I request (c), a s 148E
-- direction or order (d), a refusal on a s 152K request (e). (2): "An appeal may not be made in respect of a request
-- ... rejected by a Council because it was made during a period in which the request was not permitted". s 159B(1): a
-- practitioner "who is the subject of action taken by the Council ... under section 150, 150A or 150C may appeal, with
-- respect to a point of law". Other Council decisions: the deposit is silent (NOTHING).
@export Can I appeal to the Tribunal against this decision of the NSW Council about me (ss 159, 159B)?
GIVEN `the decision` IS A `Council decision about me` @desc The Council's decision. One of: "a reprimand under Division 3", "a suspension under Division 3, or a refusal to end one", "conditions imposed under s 127AA or Division 3 or 4, or their alteration", "a refusal of my s 150I request to alter or remove conditions", "a direction or order at an inquiry at a Council meeting (s 148E)", "a refusal of my s 152K request about impairment conditions or suspension", "a rejection of my request because it was made in a period when requests were not permitted", "another decision"
      `it is a point of law about urgent action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your appeal is only on a point of law about action the Council took under s 150, 150A or 150C (urgent suspension or conditions, their review, or their alteration)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I appeal against the Council's decision` MEANS
    IF `it is a point of law about urgent action`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE CONSIDER `the decision`
         WHEN `a rejection of my request because it was made in a period when requests were not permitted` THEN JUST FALSE
         WHEN `another decision` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: s 159 does not list it; the deposit gives no other route
         OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@export My complaint went to a Committee: can I, the practitioner, appeal to the Tribunal against its finding or orders (s 158)?
GIVEN `the deciding body` IS A `Complaint body` @desc The body that decided. One of: "a Professional Standards Committee", "an Assessment Committee", "the Tribunal", "the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I appeal against the Committee, as the practitioner` MEANS
    `may appeal against the Committee` `the deciding body`

@export Is my appeal to the Tribunal, as the practitioner, in time (s 161)?
GIVEN `the day I was given the written reasons` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given written notice of the reasons for the decision you are appealing (ISO date)
      `the day I lodged my appeal` IS A DATE @desc The day you lodged (or will lodge) your appeal (ISO date)
      `the Tribunal gave me longer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has allowed you a longer period
GIVETH AN `Appeal timing`
`Is my appeal in time, as the practitioner` MEANS
    `timing of an appeal` `the day I was given the written reasons` `the day I lodged my appeal` `the Tribunal gave me longer`

@export Can I, the practitioner, appeal now to the Tribunal on a point of law in the Committee inquiry (s 158A(2))?
GIVEN `the inquiry stage` IS A `Stage of the Committee inquiry` @desc Where the inquiry is. One of: "before notice of the inquiry was given", "after notice of the inquiry, before it began", "during the inquiry", "after the inquiry was completed"
      `the day the Committee decided the point` IS A DATE @desc During the inquiry: the day the Committee decided the point of law (ISO date; any date otherwise)
      `the day I lodge my appeal` IS A DATE @desc The day you lodge (or will lodge) the appeal (ISO date)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I appeal on a point of law now, as the practitioner` MEANS
    `point of law appeal may be made` `the inquiry stage` (DATE_SERIAL `the day I lodge my appeal` MINUS DATE_SERIAL `the day the Committee decided the point`)

-- s 161B: "An appeal under this Division does not operate to stay the effect of the decision being appealed against
-- unless the Tribunal otherwise orders." Asked as "does the decision still bind me while I appeal?"
@export I have appealed to the Tribunal: does the decision still have effect while my appeal is heard (s 161B)?
GIVEN `the Tribunal ordered a stay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has ordered that the decision be stayed while the appeal is heard
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the decision still bind me while I appeal` MEANS
    JUST (NOT `the Tribunal ordered a stay`)

§§ `Review of orders and registering again -- ss 149C(4A), 149E, 163, 163A` -- arises after a suspension, cancellation, conditions or a prohibition order

-- s 163A(1): "A person may apply to the appropriate review body for a review of (a) a prohibition order made in relation
-- to the person; or (b) a relevant order" (suspension, cancellation or disqualification, conditions); (2) also "an
-- order made under this Division". (3): it "may not be made (a) while the terms of the order provide that an
-- application for review may not be made; or (b) while an appeal to the Tribunal or the Supreme Court in respect of the
-- same matter is pending." s 163(2): lodged with the Executive Officer of the Council.
@export Can I apply now for a review of the order made against me (s 163A)? The application is lodged with the Executive Officer of the Council for your profession.
GIVEN `the order` IS AN `Order against me` @desc The order. One of: "a prohibition order", "a suspension of my registration", "a cancellation of my registration, or a disqualification from registration", "conditions on my registration", "an order made on an earlier review", "another order"
      `the order says no review yet` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order says no review application may be made until a time that has not yet come
      `an appeal on the same matter is pending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an appeal to the Tribunal or the Supreme Court about the same matter is pending
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply for review of the order` MEANS
    IF `the order says no review yet` OR `an appeal on the same matter is pending`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `the order` EQUALS `another order`
         THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: s 163A lists the orders it covers; this is not one, and the text is silent
         ELSE JUST TRUE

-- s 149E(1): a person whose registration "has been cancelled by the Tribunal", or who "has been disqualified from being
-- registered ... by the Tribunal", "cannot make an application for registration ... unless the Tribunal has made a
-- reinstatement order under section 163B"; (2) this continues "even if the period of disqualification has expired".
-- s 149C(4A): where the Tribunal decides it would have suspended a person no longer registered, "the person may not
-- apply for registration ... during the period for which the person would have been suspended". Otherwise registration
-- is under Part 7 of the National Law, not deposited: NOTHING.
@export Can I apply to be registered again after the Tribunal's decision (ss 149C(4A), 149E)?
GIVEN `the Tribunal cancelled my registration or disqualified me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal cancelled your registration, or disqualified you from being registered (even if the disqualification period has ended)
      `the Tribunal made a reinstatement order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal has made a reinstatement order for you on a review (s 163B)
      `I am within a period I would have been suspended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal decided it would have suspended you had you still been registered, and that period has not yet ended
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I apply to be registered again` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `I am within a period I would have been suspended` THEN JUST FALSE
           IF `the Tribunal cancelled my registration or disqualified me` THEN JUST `the Tribunal made a reinstatement order`
           OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind: no bar in this deposit; registration itself is under Part 7 of the National Law, not deposited

§§ `Examinations, fines and the hearing -- ss 145F, 147C(3), 146C, 148F, 149B, 147E, 148D, 165J, 171, 171B` -- arises once a complaint is made

-- s 145F: a failure "without reasonable excuse, to comply with a direction under section 145B to attend counselling or
-- under section 145E to undergo an examination is ... evidence that the practitioner ... does not have sufficient
-- physical or mental capacity to practise". s 147C(3): a failure, without reasonable excuse, to comply with a skills
-- testing notice "is ... evidence the health practitioner does not have sufficient skill to practise".
@export What follows if I did not attend counselling, an examination or skills testing the Council or Committee directed (ss 145F, 147C(3))?
GIVEN `the direction` IS A `Direction I did not follow` @desc The direction. One of: "a direction to attend counselling (s 145B(1)(g))", "a direction to undergo an examination (s 145E)", "a notice to undergo skills testing (s 147C)"
      `I complied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you complied with the direction
      `I had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you had a reasonable excuse for not complying
GIVETH AN `Effect of not complying`
`What follows if I did not comply` MEANS
    IF `I complied` OR `I had a reasonable excuse`
    THEN `the Act draws no such inference`
    ELSE IF `the direction` EQUALS `a notice to undergo skills testing (s 147C)`
         THEN `it is evidence that I lack sufficient skill to practise`
         ELSE `it is evidence that I lack sufficient physical or mental capacity to practise`

@export What is the most I can be fined over the complaint (ss 146C, 148F, 149B)? Answers in dollars; 0 where the Act bars a fine.
GIVEN `my profession` IS A `Health profession` @desc Your profession. One of: "medical", "nursing and midwifery", "pharmacy", "another registered health profession"
      `the deciding body` IS A `Complaint body` @desc The body deciding. One of: "the Tribunal", "a Professional Standards Committee", "an Assessment Committee", "the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting"
      `what was found against me` IS A `What was found` @desc What the body found, or you admitted in writing. One of: "unsatisfactory professional conduct", "professional misconduct", "another ground (competence, impairment, a conviction, suitability)", "nothing proved or admitted"
      `the body is satisfied no other order is appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the body is satisfied that no other order, or combination of orders, is appropriate in the public interest
      `a court already penalised me for it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court has already fined or penalised you for the same conduct
      `value of a penalty unit` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of a penalty unit in dollars (s 17 of the Crimes (Sentencing Procedure) Act 1999; 110 at the time of writing)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the most I can be fined` MEANS
    `maximum fine in dollars` `my profession` `the deciding body` `what was found against me` `the body is satisfied no other order is appropriate` `a court already penalised me for it` `value of a penalty unit`

@export Can I attend and have a lawyer at the hearing of the complaint about me (ss 147E, 148D(6), 165J, 171B)?
GIVEN `the deciding body` IS A `Complaint body` @desc The body hearing the complaint. One of: "the Tribunal", "a Professional Standards Committee", "an Assessment Committee", "the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting"
GIVETH A `Representation`
`Can I have a lawyer, as the practitioner` MEANS
    `representation before the body` `the deciding body` `the practitioner`

@export A Professional Standards Committee will hold an inquiry into the complaint about me: was I given enough notice (s 171(4))?
GIVEN `the day the notice reached me` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given notice of the inquiry (ISO date)
      `the inquiry day` IS A DATE @desc The day the inquiry is to be held (ISO date)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was I given enough notice, as the practitioner` MEANS
    `given enough notice of the inquiry` `the day the notice reached me` `the inquiry day`

@export Can I be ordered to pay costs in the proceedings on the complaint (Schedule 5D cl 13)?
GIVEN `the deciding body` IS A `Complaint body` @desc The body hearing the complaint. One of: "the Tribunal", "a Professional Standards Committee", "an Assessment Committee", "the Council at an inquiry at a Council meeting"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be ordered to pay costs, as the practitioner` MEANS
    `may be ordered to pay costs` `the deciding body`
